    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d313">

                <pb facs="0662" n="650" sameAs="frus:frus1946v09/pg650"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by General Marshall to General Chou
                            En-lai</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">OSE 10</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-05-03">May 3, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear General Chou</hi>: I handed a copy of your
                    letter dated 29 April 1946 concerning the Hankow situation to General Yu Ta-Wei.
                    Immediately upon arrival in Nanking, General Yu Ta-Wei contacted appropriate
                    Government officials with respect to this situation.</p>
                <p>General Yu Ta-Wei reported to me that General Ho Ying-chin<anchor n="10"
                        xml:id="d313-f1"/> has sent a message to the area to determine the facts.
                    General Yu assured me that instructions had been issued to facilitate the
                    entrance of UNRRA and CNRRA supplies into the area; and that Government forces
                    would not attack the Communists providing the Communists abided by the Cease
                    Fire Agreement and launched no attacks themselves.</p>
                <p>This same information is being furnished me by memorandum<anchor n="11"
                        xml:id="d313-f2"/> a copy of which I will forward to you upon receipt.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
